gure out how it would  work i think that was kind of the fun of  it  i'm kind of obsessed with like the  logistics of stop motion because it's  just it seems like  the hardest thing in the entire world  and i'm wondering just  what was sort of the most innocuous  thing that someone wouldn't notice in  the show that took  the most time to get right you have to  think of it like live action and modoc  is such an insane  character and design and he's you know  like just getting his chair to work  and and do what we needed it to do to be  this  this swiss army knife and and function  or like you know his arms he can't clap  you know but you write it into a script  no not class but like the way he's built  his arms don't touch so he had to like  create little close-up  you know hands and stuff and um uh you  know how does he fit into a car look at  him so we had to build  things because we have scenes in in cars  and stuff um  you know it was it was more like  practically how do these people inhabit  a world  um but you know stop motion is such a  very specific art form and it's  it's incredible and um you know watching  the craftsmanship that goes into it was  unbelievable and for me i'd come from 2d  so i had to kind of re-learn how to how  to use  to look to do this sort of animation how  do you edit this how do you  write towards this how do you get the  most out of it and um  it was it was a blast to be thrown into  the deep end and  working with with alex kamer and eric  towner who are the  directors on the show and and they  really  took me through the process so i could  understand it and you know you can even  see like you know i think  uh i'm very happy with how the pilot  came out but i learned how to edit  stop motion better as the show  progressed so i think even the cuts are  a little tighter and better and  there's more tricks you learn you know  so um  it was all it was all learning  experience in a really fun way because  you know i was on american dad for years  and you get comfortable and it's really  good to you know change it up and try  something new  absolutely um so i i don't know if you  could  tell me if there's a season like season  two is sort of i mean i don't think it's  been officially  announced i don't know uh we're we're  waiting to hear  and uh i would say if you're very  nervous about the fate of medoc's family  let hulu know  how much do you have planned for season  two if if right now they're like okay  modoc season two it's happening could  you leap right into production or is it  like oh it's  happening we gotta come up with a show  yeah no we absolutely have a shape  for what we wanna do and there's things  we set up in um  you know season one like hexis and stuff  that we want to come back to  and and address and obviously we have to  immediately address what happens in the  finale  um but um yeah no there's there's a lot  of cool stories  you know that we want to want to tell  and and we know  we've purposely left things in a certain  way with certain characters and  relationships that we want to see  uh we want to take uh with us so um yeah  there's a lot of plans in place  and and we we had some big conversations  about it kind of towards the end and  have talked to hulu about it too uh as  well and they're  really excited to know where we want to  take the show and stuff so  uh i'm hoping you know people watch it  and they want more  exactly uh something you mentioned  specifically on twitter was uh and it's  in something from the comics was  introducing uh melissa's girlfriend and  just because you sort of layer in the  fact that she's bisexual and it's just  like  wonderful little character detail that  that's just sort of in and i'm wondering  if that was to come to the show if if  that's something  that was important to you from the  beginning or is it something that  because again i i just think it's really  lovely the way it just sort of  is i love the idea that modoc is this  super villain  but having a bisexual daughter doesn't  phrase him  that's not a problem to him so i'm  wondering if that was something  conversations you had  for season one something you always plan  to reveal in season two or anything like  that  yeah it was something we always kept  trying to get to in season one the  problem is season one is so much about  modoc and everyone's relationship to  modoc that we never really get to see  melissa's life outside of um of him  and you know there was moments where we  tried to work it in like you know in the  pilot  uh she holds up a dead boyfriend and  we're like i don't want to like  introduce this relationship and then the  first thing we do is murder  the person you know and you know there  were lines and stuff  that were always in there so um i was  like i want to tell the story until  right and i was very annoyed that we  didn't get to it was just kind of  you're constantly cutting things for  time and you know and really  the her story with modoc was about them  you know and not her life outside of it  but it was always something that was  like  i wanted to get to that um because you  know representation is very important  for me  in in the show so we were so happy that  we were able to kind of  canonize it in the comic and um  and so was melissa fumer was so excited  i actually bought that  page for her and gave it to her so she  has that  that one um so uh yeah it's something  that was like  we absolutely want to do and the same  thing with lou like we really didn't get  to see his life  you know too much outside of modok jody  we only got that kind of  one episode with her and you know wonder  man where  i i would love to just be able to now  you know  not leave mark behind but introduce  these characters that you  love them that you're you want to go on  journeys with them into their own worlds  as well  outside of how they relate to modak so  that was something that we absolutely  want to tackle  in season two uh with should the family  survive  um yeah but uh yeah yeah  it's it's a it's a big thing for me i  think you know representation was also  built into  the family itself is we wanted to to  showcase a modern  family that feels real you know and i'm  in an interfaith  relationship or marriage and that leads  to a lot of interesting stories and the  same thing of  having um you know a mixed family where  modoc is jewish and uh you know um  uh jody is mexican and having uh  a son having a bar mitzvah being able to  tell my story you know that was just  something we really wanted to do because  there are so many just all white you  know  animated families and the last thing we  wanted to do was kind of add  to that as opposed to be able to create  a family that feels new and  and different and unique and and kind of  representative of what  families look like now absolutely  besides the giant heads  yeah you know the core is there the  chorus yeah  yeah again thank you for taking the time  and just thank you for the show in  general because again i never thought i  would see a modok show  much less like i'm on a major stream if  i were so again this is a  this was a treat thank you thank you for  for  i love that a modoc fan enjoyed it that  means a lot to me
 

